"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[62312],{262312:(e,t,n)=>{n.r(t),n.d(t,{tutorial:()=>a}),n(692738);let a={id:"build-data-pipeline",title:"Build a data pipeline",description:"Learn how to create and manage data pipelines in Databricks from start to finish.",totalDuration:"13m",category:"data-pipeline",steps:[{id:"step-1",componentId:"homepage.tutorials.build-data-pipeline.what-is-a-pipeline",title:"What is a pipeline?",description:"Learn the fundamentals of data pipelines, the medallion architecture, and how declarative pipelines simplify data engineering in Databricks.",duration:"4m",videoUrl:"https://player.vimeo.com/progressive_redirect/playback/1144698000/rendition/720p/file.mp4?loc=external&signature=8e54d58ec8cb345ffb3e7c0263da18674cc12ae6a953c18e412cff878806acd2",vimeoId:"1144698000",thumbnailUrl:n.p+"static/media/step1.ef47b26ad6642528e7971ead697828e1.svg",contentType:"Video",order:1,sections:[{id:"section-1",title:"TL;DR",content:"- A Databricks pipeline automates turning raw data into clean, ready-to-use tables.\n- Pipelines replace manual notebook reruns with scheduled or triggered execution.\n- The medallion architecture organizes data into bronze (raw), silver (clean), and gold (business-ready).\n- Declarative pipelines let you define *what* you want, and Databricks handles the orchestration.\n- This reduces custom code, errors, and maintenance while keeping data continuously fresh.",order:1},{id:"section-2",title:"What This Is / Why It Matters",content:"A Databricks pipeline is an automated workflow that turns messy input data into structured, business-ready tables. Instead of manually running notebooks or scripts every day, the pipeline does this work continuously and reliably.\n\nThis matters because most analytics and ML workflows depend on consistent, fresh data. Manual execution is error-prone and doesn't scale. Pipelines solve this by enforcing an ordered, repeatable process.\n\nThe intuition is simple: treat your data processing like a factory line. Raw materials go in, transformations happen in the middle, and finished products come out. Pipelines formalize this process so it's predictable and hands-off.\n\nDeclarative pipelines take this further: you specify the tables you want, and Databricks figures out the execution plan. This removes the need for custom orchestration code and makes pipelines dramatically simpler to build and maintain.",order:2},{id:"section-3",title:"What a Pipeline Is",content:"**Concept & Why It Matters**\nA pipeline is an automated workflow that processes data end-to-end. It ensures data is always up to date without manual execution.\nRead more: [Databricks Workflows](https://docs.databricks.com/workflows/)\n\n**What the Video Demonstrates**\n- A pipeline takes raw, messy data and outputs clean, business-ready tables.\n- It runs automatically on a schedule or when new data arrives.\n- This replaces daily manual notebook execution.",order:3},{id:"section-4",title:"Viewing a Pipeline as a Factory Line",content:"**Concept & Why It Matters**\nPipelines can be understood as a factory process for data:\n- Inputs: files, logs, exports (batch or streaming)\n- Middle: cleaning, joins, transformations\n- Output: finished analytics tables\n\nThis mental model helps engineers visualize how data flows through processing layers.\n\n**What the Video Demonstrates**\n- Raw data enters at the start.\n- Transformations occur in sequence.\n- Final tables power dashboards, reports, and ML models.\n- The pipeline orchestrates all steps automatically in the correct order.",order:4},{id:"section-5",title:"The Medallion Architecture",content:"**Concept & Why It Matters**\nThe medallion architecture organizes data into Bronze → Silver → Gold layers, making pipelines easier to reason about and maintain.\nSee: [Medallion Architecture](https://docs.databricks.com/lakehouse/medallion-architecture.html)\n\n- **Bronze:** Raw ingested data\n- **Silver:** Cleaned and structured\n- **Gold:** Aggregated business-level tables\n\nEach layer builds on the previous one, creating a scalable and predictable data flow.\n\n**What the Video Demonstrates**\n- Bronze holds raw data as-is in Delta Lake.\n- Silver cleans and deduplicates it.\n- Gold aggregates it for analytics, BI, ML, and streaming use cases.",order:5},{id:"section-6",title:"Example: Online Store Analytics",content:"**Concept & Why It Matters**\nExamples make pipelines concrete. For an online store, daily reporting depends on consistently processed data.\n\n**What the Video Demonstrates**\n- Raw orders, customers, and product data land in Bronze.\n- Silver cleans data and removes duplicates.\n- Gold produces:\n  - Daily orders\n  - Revenue by country\n  - Top products\n- Dashboards pull from Gold tables and stay fresh automatically.",order:6},{id:"section-7",title:"The Challenge: Building Pipelines by Hand Is Hard",content:"**Concept & Why It Matters**\nTraditional pipelines require extensive custom engineering around:\n- Orchestration\n- Version control\n- Deployment\n- Quality checks\n- Governance\n- Backfilling\n- Dependencies\n- Checkpointing and partitions\n\nThis overhead distracts from the core task: transforming data.\n\n**What the Video Demonstrates**\n- Most pipeline complexity is not business-specific.\n- Manual solutions require large amounts of glue code.\n- This leads to reliability issues and slow iteration.",order:7},{id:"section-8",title:"Declarative Pipelines",content:"**Concept & Why It Matters**\nDeclarative pipelines let you specify *what* the final tables should look like, not *how* to orchestrate the steps. Databricks handles the details.\nLearn more: [Delta Live Tables](https://docs.databricks.com/delta-live-tables/index.html)\n\n**What the Video Demonstrates**\n- You define:\n  - Input data\n  - Cleaned tables\n  - Final business tables\n- Databricks determines:\n  - Execution order\n  - What needs updating\n  - Efficient processing\n  - Safe, reliable operations\n- Benefits:\n  - Less manual work\n  - Fewer errors\n  - Faster iteration\n  - Always-fresh data",order:8},{id:"section-9",title:"Summary",content:"The video concludes by noting that pipelines automate turning raw data into clean, business-ready tables. Declarative pipelines make the process dramatically simpler by letting Databricks handle orchestration. The next video will walk through building such a pipeline step by step.",order:9},{id:"section-10",title:"Key Takeaways",content:"- Pipelines automate transforming raw data into structured analytics tables.\n- The medallion architecture organizes data into Bronze → Silver → Gold for clarity and reliability.\n- Dashboards and ML models depend on the Gold layer for fresh, consistent data.\n- Building pipelines manually is complex due to orchestration and dependency challenges.\n- Declarative pipelines reduce custom code and operational overhead.\n- Databricks handles ordering, updates, and efficient processing for you.\n- The result is faster iteration, better reliability, and always up-to-date data.",order:10}]},{id:"step-2",componentId:"homepage.tutorials.build-data-pipeline.explore-a-sample-pipeline",title:"Explore a sample pipeline",description:"Explore a sample declarative pipeline that demonstrates how to build and manage pipelines using Databricks.",duration:"7m",videoUrl:"https://player.vimeo.com/progressive_redirect/playback/1144384900/rendition/1080p/file.mp4?loc=external&signature=7e12173f5e6fc4d23846f00ffe61317d55c92aee7a5cc806cb2569aa5262695b",vimeoId:"1144384900",thumbnailUrl:n.p+"static/media/step2.2b9e2d05b9538a3d5280b23d3e7f6452.svg",contentType:"Video and demo",requirements:"Explore this demo in your workspace:\n\n1. Click **New** in the top left\n2. Create a notebook in a new tab\n3. Copy this code into a notebook cell\n\n```python\n%pip install dbdemos -U\nimport dbdemos\ndbdemos.install('pipeline-bike', catalog='main', schema='dbdemos_pipeline_bike')\n```\n\n4. Run the cell (this can take a couple of minutes)\n\nThis automatically creates synthetic data for every source table in the project - rides, maintenance logs, customers, and more. It allows the entire pipeline to run end-to-end in your free workspace.",notebook:{name:"Bike Demo Installation",language:"python",cells:[{type:"markdown",content:"# Bike Demo Installation\n\nThis notebook installs the bike demo dataset and pipeline. Run the cell below to get started.\n\n**Tip:** Press `Cmd + Enter` (Mac) or `Ctrl + Enter` (Windows/Linux) to run a cell."},{type:"code",content:"%pip install dbdemos -U\nimport dbdemos\ndbdemos.install('pipeline-bike', catalog='main', schema='dbdemos_pipeline_bike')"}]},order:2,sections:[{id:"section-0",title:"TL;DR",content:"- Declarative pipelines let you define *what* tables should exist, and Databricks automatically figures out *how* to build, run, and update them.\n- Autoloader + streaming tables turn cloud storage into a continuous, incremental data source with no ingestion infrastructure to manage.\n- The Pipeline Editor (DAG, table inspector, asset browser) gives you a fast, intuitive workspace for developing, testing, and debugging pipelines.\n- The Medallion layout (Bronze → Silver → Gold) organizes your work so raw data, cleaned data, and business metrics each have a clear place.\n- Materialized views, constraints, and AI functions show how Databricks automates incremental computation, data quality, and text structuring for you.",order:0},{id:"section-1",title:"What This Pipeline Represents",content:"The pipeline models a fictional bike rental business and demonstrates how to build and manage pipelines using Databricks.\n\nIt uses the **Medallion Architecture**, which organizes data into three layers:\n\n- **Bronze**: raw ingestion\n- **Silver**: cleaned and structured\n- **Gold**: aggregated and optimized for reporting\n\nIf you're new to Medallion, see the developer doc for [Medallion Architecture](https://docs.databricks.com/en/lakehouse/medallion.html).\n\nThis structure isn't just conceptual—it directly influences where you place transformations, quality checks, and business logic.",order:1},{id:"section-2",title:"Editing Experience (Pipeline Editor)",content:'The Pipeline Editor is your primary workspace for building and iterating on this demo.\n\n### Key interface areas\n\n- **DAG (right side)**: A visual map of table dependencies and lineage. See the developer doc for [Declarative Pipelines](https://docs.databricks.com/en/dev-tools/pipelines/index.html).\n- **Table Inspector (bottom)**: Schema preview, data sample, run details.\n- **Pipeline Asset Browser (left)**: Organized project files—SQL, Python, notebooks, tests, and documentation.\n- **Tabs (top)**: Quick navigation between files.\n\n## What "Declarative" Means in Practice\n\nInstead of building jobs, notebooks, orchestration logic, and incremental processing manually, you:\n\n1. Describe the *tables* you want.\n2. Define the *logic* that produces them.\n3. Databricks:\n   - Builds the DAG\n   - Determines execution order\n   - Handles incremental updates\n   - Optimizes performance\n\nSee the developer doc for [Declarative Pipelines](https://docs.databricks.com/en/dev-tools/pipelines/index.html).\n\nThis frees you to think about *data logic*, not scheduling or orchestration.\n\n## Pipeline Asset Browser Details\n\nThe sample project includes:\n\n- `exploration/` for notebooks and ad-hoc analysis\n- `transformation/` for SQL and Python transformations\n- Additional helper files (docs, tests) marked with a "not executed" icon\n\nYou can execute a single table definition directly in the editor, which is a fast way to debug transformations before running the entire DAG.\n\nNotebooks remain useful for interactive exploration and lightweight documentation.',order:2},{id:"section-3",title:"Data Exploration Notebook",content:"The exploration notebook is your first look at the raw data and exposes typical ingestion issues.\n\n### Things you learn from exploring the raw data:\n\n- The pipeline consumes daily Parquet, CSV, and JSON files stored in a volume.\n- Filenames include timestamps, indicating a daily ingestion pattern.\n- The `issue_description` column in maintenance logs contains **newline characters**, which break CSV/JSON parsing.\n\n### Fixing the parsing issue\n\nTo correctly parse multiline records, set `multi-line = true` in the read function. This eliminates nulls and misaligned rows and ensures the data loads cleanly.",order:3},{id:"section-4",title:"Bronze Layer (Raw Ingestion)",content:"The Bronze layer defines ingestion as **streaming tables**, which continuously pick up new data as it lands.\n\n## What a Streaming Table Is\n\nA streaming table is a continuously updated table that processes new data automatically. It's ideal when data arrives in cloud storage or Kafka and you want a simple, incremental ingest path. See the developer doc for [Streaming Tables](https://docs.databricks.com/en/delta-live-tables/concepts-streaming-tables.html).\n\n## How Autoloader Fits In\n\nBronze tables use the `STREAM` keyword with `read_files`, which activates **Autoloader**, an optimized ingestion service that:\n\n- Detects new files as they arrive\n- Infers schema automatically\n- Evolves schema as needed\n- Processes data incrementally\n\nSee the developer doc for [Autoloader](https://docs.databricks.com/en/ingestion/auto-loader/index.html).\n\nAutoloader eliminates the need to build file scanners or manual incremental logic.",order:4},{id:"section-5",title:"Silver Layer (Transformation & Quality)",content:"Silver tables refine the Bronze data into structured, trustworthy datasets.\n\n### Key techniques introduced here:\n\n**1. Constraints (data quality rules)**  \nConstraints allow you to validate and clean data automatically. Example: A recent run dropped 334 invalid rows from the rides table. See the developer doc for [Data Quality Constraints](https://docs.databricks.com/en/clean-rooms/run-data-quality.html).\n\n**2. AI Classification**  \nThe `AI_CLASSIFY` function converts messy free text into standardized categories using language models built into Databricks. See the developer doc for [AI Functions](https://docs.databricks.com/en/large-language-models/functions/index.html).\n\nThe result is structured, high-quality, analysis-ready data.",order:5},{id:"section-6",title:"Gold Layer (Aggregations & Business Metrics)",content:"The Gold layer builds business-level aggregates using **Materialized Views**.\n\n## What a Materialized View Is\n\nA materialized view stores the result of a query and updates incrementally as source data changes. It gives you query speed + automatically maintained freshness. See the developer doc for [Materialized Views](https://docs.databricks.com/en/sql/language-manual/materialized-views.html).\n\n## How the Enzyme Engine Helps\n\nDatabricks' Enzyme Engine automatically maintains the materialized view, applies incremental updates efficiently, avoids unnecessary recomputation, and optimizes performance.\n\nThis means you write a simple SQL query, and Databricks handles the complexity of incremental aggregation.",order:6},{id:"section-7",title:"Dashboards",content:"The demo includes dashboards that show:\n\n- Business insights (revenue, forecasting)\n- Data quality trends (constraints and expectations)\n- Operational health (run times, costs)\n\nThese demonstrate how pipeline outputs can feed analytics and monitoring immediately.",order:7},{id:"section-8",title:"Key Takeaways",content:"- Declarative pipelines focus your work on logic, not orchestration.\n- Autoloader and streaming tables simplify ingestion dramatically.\n- The Pipeline Editor accelerates iteration and debugging.\n- Silver transformations show where to enforce data quality and apply AI enrichment.\n- Gold tables showcase fully automated, incremental analytics using Materialized Views + Enzyme.",order:8}]},{id:"step-3",componentId:"homepage.tutorials.build-data-pipeline.build-your-own-pipeline",title:"Build your own pipeline",description:"Step-by-step guide to creating your first data pipeline using the Lakeflow Pipeline Editor.",duration:"5m",videoUrl:"https://player.vimeo.com/progressive_redirect/playback/1144563658/rendition/1080p/file.mp4?loc=external&signature=913c1ec86e1280579a174c4113979ffd065676bee8646a7014a1810e96cb0be5",vimeoId:"1144563658",thumbnailUrl:n.p+"static/media/step3.5bc9f87245a33f2c464a4b81683338aa.svg",contentType:"Video and tutorial",order:3,sections:[{id:"section-1",title:"The Lakeflow Pipeline Editor",content:"Before writing code, it helps to understand the interface you'll be working in.\n\nThe [Lakeflow pipeline editor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/multi-file-editor) is an IDE designed for building data pipelines. It has five main areas:\n\n- **Asset browser (left):** Lists all source files in your pipeline\n- **File editor (center):** Where you write pipeline code\n- **Pipeline toolbar (top right):** Configure settings and run the full pipeline\n- **Pipeline graph (right):** Visualizes the DAG — all tables and their relationships\n- **Table insights (bottom):** Shows results, previews, and errors after runs",order:1},{id:"section-2",title:"Step 1: Configure the Pipeline Environment",content:"Before writing pipeline code, you need to install any external packages your pipeline depends on.\n\nThe `pyspark-data-sources` package provides a simple interface for streaming data from the OpenSky API. To add it:\n\n1. Click **Settings** in the pipeline toolbar\n2. Scroll to **Pipeline Environment** and click **Edit Environment**\n3. Add `pyspark-data-sources` as a dependency\n4. Click **Save**\n\nThis is equivalent to running `pip install` inside your pipeline's environment.",order:2},{id:"section-3",title:"Step 2: Write the Ingest Step",content:'Rename the default `my_transformation.py` file to `ingest_flights.py`.\n\n### Code\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark import pipelines as dp\nfrom pyspark_datasources import OpenSkyDataSource\nspark.dataSource.register(OpenSkyDataSource)\n\n@dp.table\ndef ingest_flights():\n    return spark.readStream.format("opensky").load()\n```',order:3},{id:"section-4",title:"Step 3: Write the Transform Step",content:'Create a new file called `flights_stats.py`.\n\n### Code\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark import pipelines as dp\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import *\n\n@dp.table\ndef flights_stats():\n    df = spark.read.table("ingest_flights")\n    return (\n        df.agg(\n            count("*").alias("num_events"),\n            countDistinct("icao24").alias("distinct_aircraft"),\n            max("velocity").alias("max_velocity"),\n        )\n    )\n```',order:4},{id:"section-5",title:"Next steps",content:"1. Try modifying the transformation logic\n2. Add an additional step to the pipeline\n3. Build a dashboard to visualize the flight statistics",order:5}]}]}}}]);
